on has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x\\in R,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186384719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f828cb-c8b1-5da4-9711-15ace712dffa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Vacuous Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c158e2-390a-5c35-9880-ef8cf68fdeb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186384721} {:answer/id #uuid "16804d9e-c648-5f7e-8892-bf7ae003a226" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186384722} {:answer/id #uuid "e61344da-f77b-5548-8382-dcdaa77dd110" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186384723} {:answer/id #uuid "ded87ef1-c134-51ea-9887-4c8adb9c7e33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186384724} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c5e6ba-ecfb-554d-b0d7-0735ac02b226" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186384725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61344da-f77b-5548-8382-dcdaa77dd110" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186384723} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ab59b5-cc23-50ae-9b78-4cb6f1002df3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186384720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abf1ead3-58c2-53df-80ac-6b0af0110700" :question/math-academy-id "q-216046" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then ${n}^{2}<0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $n+1>n$\n- $\\sqrt{n}\\in Q$\n- $0<n<1$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186384726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f828cb-c8b1-5da4-9711-15ace712dffa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Vacuous Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6348627-1d11-5d72-b0d1-1d5afec7b772" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186384728} {:answer/id #uuid "44fa9070-c093-5a41-9f15-e3ec4b9dfbb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186384729} {:answer/id #uuid "3f165b25-9cea-5606-96bf-a535d29fafda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186384730} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4bbf0b-c23c-54fa-942b-41931fe828fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186384731} {:answer/id #uuid "3b51f7e7-ca8f-51f8-a8eb-a0c759c5a16d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186384732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b51f7e7-ca8f-51f8-a8eb-a0c759c5a16d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186384732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f87852-c895-5147-ac53-ddc4476d7d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186384727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba9ef88d-4295-592b-9071-b4477cb43227" :question/math-academy-id "q-216054" :question/problem "Let $x\\in Q.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $|x+3|<8.$ \n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $\\sqrt{5}\\in R$\n- ${cos}^{2}⁡x>1$\n- $3x+2>0$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bce6d6cd-325a-5233-b2a2-7df246b87b60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385180} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9a9c9c-77dc-5bae-a705-011d19c4f496" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186385181} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd7c2d4-77c3-5de5-a3b9-dbb265a5ac3a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385182} {:answer/id #uuid "76dfc80a-22e0-50c5-8464-eb495771ecc6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186385183} {:answer/id #uuid "b39bf3fc-c9dd-58de-9105-e0975804b524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186385184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b39bf3fc-c9dd-58de-9105-e0975804b524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186385184} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6b2119-fae8-58ab-8eef-754e579be530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7019503-fd19-5657-a7e0-238936f56d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-334241" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186385612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5d145da-97ce-547e-a753-1a83cee2cff3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385614} {:answer/id #uuid "cd76936e-f041-580d-8d47-910ac26ba515" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385615} {:answer/id #uuid "32e81036-11a9-565b-bcd3-4fe2fe9eb20e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385616} {:answer/id #uuid "956ffac4-21fd-51f0-b89d-d174fd85de32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385617} {:answer/id #uuid "97a98d26-73da-54aa-be15-fe6ff504cf76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956ffac4-21fd-51f0-b89d-d174fd85de32" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385617} :answer-field/id #uuid "762e03d4-09ef-50a3-abcb-d5091d662911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c69759b-6bd0-5ee4-a581-0fdeb88d4d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76241" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:[0,2\\pi ]\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\n- III is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186385638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d476250b-936b-59a4-989c-bb04ac46d79d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385640} {:answer/id #uuid "88ec0c4c-63d8-5a82-acd3-403167b0c8e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385641} {:answer/id #uuid "8cac40e1-6aa5-5a30-a598-266d6e10f1b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385642} {:answer/id #uuid "9a90c907-263f-5c92-a65b-18d4116ec2f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385643} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f757fe-bf63-55b0-9c6b-666d2da5b195" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cac40e1-6aa5-5a30-a598-266d6e10f1b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385642} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c51726e-f69d-556c-9121-188dd98391fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03ec5353-d045-5242-94cf-0882c6a84756" :question/math-academy-id "q-77455" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{1,2,3,4}\\to {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- $g:{-4,-2,0,2,4,8,10}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,0,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not* an injection. For example, $g(-4)=16=g(4),$ but $-4\\ne 4.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(1),$ but $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186385645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5273ce7-e84c-543e-bd20-674a98e65b0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385647} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e84721-013c-5dab-a69e-82c411cf0006" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385648} {:answer/id #uuid "5d31af35-5d65-5adf-9858-1bc955c652dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385649} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7fbe82-61d9-536f-8d91-395851ee6150" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385650} {:answer/id #uuid "e723d2d6-4c15-54b6-81b2-89fc8c381dc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e723d2d6-4c15-54b6-81b2-89fc8c381dc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e31cc959-bec9-528e-b368-de6a5467600d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75f6f36b-64d5-52cc-809a-ed8ce71cf663" :question/math-academy-id "q-77463" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{1,3,5,7,9}\\to {-4,-2,0,12}$ defined by $f(x)=(x-3)(x-7)$\n- $g:{1,2,3,4,5}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(3)=0=f(7),$ but $3\\ne 7.$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186385652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fdb96f4-2021-5751-80da-5b36eba1c5d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385654} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cf459e-1c5f-55aa-b32e-e6f27d973712" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385655} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1fe5e0-d060-5bf7-b7b8-af59cd760672" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385656} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb7581c-d0d2-537a-a6f7-c3e316402841" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385657} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7583c0-458f-586c-b7b4-f4f7d74d0eeb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bb7581c-d0d2-537a-a6f7-c3e316402841" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385657} :answer-field/id #uuid "da13ca19-0d2d-5bd1-810f-7ea3a2872fc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3065c9f8-73e6-55e4-8dcd-b18452c2889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-77465" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:{-1,0,1,2,3}\\to Z$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g:{-2,-1,0,1,2}\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $h:Z\\to {-1,1}$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2),$ but $0\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186385659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37687926-8f0a-55b2-83ff-19bf952a848e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385661} {:answer/id #uuid "43bdea7f-ce92-58be-a635-2f4a1e204def" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385662} {:answer/id #uuid "595dcb01-bbd6-5210-ab29-62ed9755bcf8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385663} {:answer/id #uuid "d09d989e-e4f6-5f62-a325-c1ab71cb28fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385664} {:answer/id #uuid "60d3f75f-6de2-582c-b480-08e1c57e99b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d3f75f-6de2-582c-b480-08e1c57e99b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385665} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fb7149-2f4c-5ed2-99b2-de2082bfaad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93abda0d-dd4f-575f-bfb4-2c2551c6ca10" :question/math-academy-id "q-77821" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=|x|$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)=4(x-2)$\n- $h:Q\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186385666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed1627e-3a9b-545f-a470-f70f3b412422" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385668} {:answer/id #uuid "961a0f6b-ba1a-5a8a-ab23-6a9b3c7a685e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385669} {:answer/id #uuid "b8284321-e3c3-5cab-b1f5-2b86d60a89aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385670} {:answer/id #uuid "811cd301-33d8-55d2-9cba-e1a1280a5c79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385671} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ae5864-50c4-57c3-99cf-642bad9d32d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8284321-e3c3-5cab-b1f5-2b86d60a89aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc8c3ae-0777-508b-b857-bfead8e728a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6066c3ca-d4e8-51fc-956b-cd9926b46fbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80083" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $2$ is even, then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $3$ is odd\n- $4$ is odd\n- $n$ is odd" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0c3f959-5171-59ff-838d-51e3fe713293" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385687} {:answer/id #uuid "a30df9d4-c782-52c3-84d6-c86e15e7d455" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385688} {:answer/id #uuid "04338b5f-5888-5616-8ee6-13a24f4daad1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385689} {:answer/id #uuid "430f4a78-64b1-52ba-a7c6-ccdf11ed801d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385690} {:answer/id #uuid "ae89a223-8688-5ddc-8a2f-de64b6ac2023" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c3f959-5171-59ff-838d-51e3fe713293" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385687} :answer-field/id #uuid "df761ac9-90f4-5c6f-ba68-ad1faf9f4e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4333b20-5791-5886-a0e1-73362bfffb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-80365" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3caf194f47cbc3fe36f969442df48e0a133ddbf77a9177e2e5e96822463c8a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(0)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(0)=9$ (we have an arrow $0\\in X$ to $9\\in Y$) and $f(1)=0$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $0\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355cb717-f2f7-5938-8e6d-fccd5ad1370b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385694} {:answer/id #uuid "04266d4e-24fd-5e94-be7c-9ac7f473a6fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385695} {:answer/id #uuid "54b387d6-9737-5f35-be3e-eea0e72ba83e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385696} {:answer/id #uuid "903c5ed8-6223-5d82-8334-35b36775775e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385697} {:answer/id #uuid "acdbd104-53bf-5b27-9c70-ebe287beff57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355cb717-f2f7-5938-8e6d-fccd5ad1370b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385694} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed99050-40cd-578a-8860-47f97fead64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf90a3c5-cc95-537c-9537-397ca87cb6b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80368" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e187742134e111e2f539fdce556c50445cc1ee8d8c6cdb93077192c14b36dfaf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f(-5)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(-5)=3$ (we have an arrow from $-5\\in X$ to $3\\in Y$) and $f(0)=-5$ (we have an arrow from $0\\in X$ to $-5\\in Y$).\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "762cec2e-1e34-5745-8646-3ae54c1bb5e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385701} {:answer/id #uuid "58dd3238-45bd-5ebe-adaa-b9eed7d9c3ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385702} {:answer/id #uuid "7c303965-9eb2-5737-b562-c41408d03d85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385703} {:answer/id #uuid "8071edd2-3b50-58f1-9f34-2086e3ef4cd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385704} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed04bf2-6eb3-5674-8b0a-fbcffc8e92a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c303965-9eb2-5737-b562-c41408d03d85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385703} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c7273e-f40a-5fe3-9767-c03dc614a22c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0677b653-a796-5407-9e1c-60e17e55a06b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80375" :question/problem "![](images/30/309253d22ab9eaf17aeb84d504c976f0c0aeab5c8bc042f686dd69d835d53705.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-1)=f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(-1)=f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-1)=f(1)$ but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ffb7a3-e0c9-5b3d-b5d4-f5aba8e22d23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385708} {:answer/id #uuid "4eae2857-be44-5148-a73c-dc3aa950ed44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385709} {:answer/id #uuid "39096522-7d0f-5710-a2a0-0806c8df2a7e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385710} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf6393-d2e5-53cd-9c60-b420c1e586aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385711} {:answer/id #uuid "151e8bdb-a031-5ed4-befa-ca0f4c6ff1f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ffb7a3-e0c9-5b3d-b5d4-f5aba8e22d23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385708} :answer-field/id #uuid "2289c275-f115-5326-8e97-b8a4a13e9e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb0a5436-5069-574d-ac30-ea6987db593d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80376" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d81a4317d789968de29f34b42358d44e74c8bbe4717467a4c780beb633dc0d5.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(1)=-1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(1)$ but $-2\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2e3bd4-c605-5900-89a7-c42b04f70825" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385729} {:answer/id #uuid "65ba502e-895c-5928-81f8-eda852b62fdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385730} {:answer/id #uuid "94735337-c07e-5e57-a4ea-21e21bec7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186385731} {:answer/id #uuid "39c845c9-a1cc-5970-ac2c-329b8395e9c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385732} {:answer/id #uuid "5eae2762-adfc-5bd6-b11b-583b65dbb707" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65ba502e-895c-5928-81f8-eda852b62fdb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385730} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26db430-4903-5213-a3cd-614fd0d112cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a04c07f-9732-5fba-9678-462e7c23fd4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80387" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[1,\\infty )\\to [0,\\infty )$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n- $f(1)\\ne f(2)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/ae/aeda30e69b9bc433e5f345822d4221e194981ce132a022f28632814376c5910e.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=(1-1)^{2}=0$ and $f(2)=(2-1)^{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[1,\\infty )$ that map to the same element in $[0,\\infty ).$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c06214-2610-5c0b-af4c-76319cd294f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385770} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4c7672-15c0-508f-99b1-e2ad662ac84e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186385771} {:answer/id #uuid "f141b7df-d2b4-5a9a-b43c-9d096a0472a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385772} {:answer/id #uuid "32cbbca9-9f7c-5c18-9146-8abe005299c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385773} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf06603-aa20-57d2-b5ae-904b39a1373a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bf06603-aa20-57d2-b5ae-904b39a1373a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385774} :answer-field/id #uuid "9179f998-6f44-58a9-800b-79c989241d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e966053-1b79-5d5f-9b9a-6962c445b14d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82293" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd6de23e9b33d0e86a91eb4bf6b81a9d443c1b389182f1c05bf10d048f0c0402.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider $y=5.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $5\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false,  $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4908f3-1701-512c-917e-6ac6928983cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385777} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5d1c-6ecb-5dac-93b2-900d09943de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385778} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ea745f-1def-5066-9449-27c1819df19c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385779} {:answer/id #uuid "8809d663-38fa-5805-a29e-a896c62ade4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385780} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb720ca-da9b-5c5c-8fcf-34d156601d0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5d1c-6ecb-5dac-93b2-900d09943de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385778} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea10d5ad-68c8-5c6c-bfdd-d759d3732695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3819ae9-243b-5030-9bcc-f473557d3d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-82297" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbe19e6a595d7b48e7f18382ed0a66452ba28c86925edad81a296cb7dd26a77f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc68439-4269-5143-aa3a-d59f65c6cb4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385784} {:answer/id #uuid "22b189df-e4b9-55d3-ba7e-84c5d0f7344f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385785} {:answer/id #uuid "cac36333-062a-5c01-beb7-0a68120274d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385786} {:answer/id #uuid "109ee471-5373-5d42-b325-1f0a3d78df64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385787} {:answer/id #uuid "f114de34-e17d-58b1-842f-0c3c85bc7ea1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc68439-4269-5143-aa3a-d59f65c6cb4b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385784} :answer-field/id #uuid "7623a998-0d72-54c2-9646-9cc62428ba4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da5d5f20-c614-5648-b2cd-38bf72010ec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-82314" :question/problem "![](images/12/126abe41ac185c082d8cc28628e2db4b2708dd84ba351718184d949ddf582d3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider $y=2.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $2\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(1)=f(4)$ but $1\\ne 4.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false (as well as statement II), $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15173f61-f9d1-538d-9291-56ec49ac1de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385791} {:answer/id #uuid "de21f12b-76b9-5dd9-8b6b-4939a3d473a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385792} {:answer/id #uuid "a8439b65-3217-53c3-9137-eb90c66b6a12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385793} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e2edd1-553f-5eda-89d5-cdae2992b045" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385794} {:answer/id #uuid "08f63530-ab2e-5ef6-919c-9fb183096d68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15173f61-f9d1-538d-9291-56ec49ac1de1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385791} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c294b2-aa91-563d-8404-3702314f59b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24770296-d90b-538e-85f2-59d06d26123a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82317" :question/problem "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=f(2\\pi )$, but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e71b74bd-cc9c-511b-bb08-2b9f56a250fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385798} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9aea36-733a-51bc-b18b-0b26a2d9673d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II,  and III" :db/id 17592186385799} {:answer/id #uuid "b129e975-80f6-57ec-8873-fb500294a55e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385800} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4b33e8-1a3e-55c8-8673-41ad6fc6cb22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385801} {:answer/id #uuid "027d9910-233d-5b2d-b0fe-5dcb4f9d8ce0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71b74bd-cc9c-511b-bb08-2b9f56a250fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186385798} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a2ac1a-f01f-5d3a-9675-3ae7580fc347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3380502f-56f6-501e-b560-ed1186ab069f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82324" :question/problem "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}-1,$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(-1)=0=f(1)$, but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186385810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b080e2e-f5e2-5ee4-82f1-8f6fef4fe4f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186385812} {:answer/id #uuid "68efaee3-df59-51e4-9e20-4036ba61ba02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385813} {:answer/id #uuid "d14a8dc4-7d2f-5022-8183-19b4122cdcb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186385814} {:answer/id #uuid "7c019049-cb0f-5c47-ba6d-b914989d03f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186385815} {:answer/id #uuid "097a71d5-c44f-5ba8-8813-7ecb5ddfbc04" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186385816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68efaee3-df59-51e4-9e20-4036ba61ba02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186385813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b445f9f3-69f5-569d-bf2e-b4c1d23b8879" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186385811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94a22ac-b31f-54dc-b35c-ba2dd08bc10e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82327" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef54b0fed6fa137d1a69b35c9b0231bf21192ca062fef6246df86a32591d0c0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={e}^{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/bd/bda8097fd869dfc60ccdd2dd42d38072248ad91a94853474461d0454c30e8027.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186390205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f9d353d-65eb-5da3-aee7-94de17e15ccd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186390207} {:answer/id #uuid "2f86a433-c584-57f5-bc5b-0e6c2efc7647" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186390208} {:answer/id #uuid "1de27e93-c301-51e9-9d79-e1cda27fe190" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186390209} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9aca34-01b7-5a12-bf90-f2948eaa27d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186390210} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7252e8-288b-5997-838c-178333ef8643" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186390211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de27e93-c301-51e9-9d79-e1cda27fe190" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186390209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c76159e-4765-5e78-b848-edf1963706a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186390206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cba8b56-135d-52f2-8f27-ede5f8ef30f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183619" :question/problem "$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-x=w\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃x$ that uses $x:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,x\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃x}{\\overset{⏞}{2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}=w}}\n$$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $w$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $w.$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,2-{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}={w}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186390212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051e28ef-ac7f-5b29-bd93-c1e24a4bc699" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Free and Bound Occurrences of Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb42914-8086-5294-8034-099af7e76c6a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186390214} {:answer/id #uuid "db29159c-b2eb-5123-82b3-4bed028db7e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186390215} {:answer/id #uuid "0c70261c-5553-516e-bab7-fda36d9236d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186390216} {:answer/id #uuid "a81762f2-7d2d-5776-86ac-47e142c084eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186390217} {:answer/id #uuid "a38367c0-0768-51e8-9758-f6b145bcb172" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186390218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a38367c0-0768-51e8-9758-f6b145bcb172" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186390218} :answer-field/id #uuid "d971224e-33f2-5800-afc5-8c6a88c45853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186390213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55254c80-6a13-5f2c-a4bb-f9bcddde8c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183649" :question/problem "$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{I}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{II}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{III}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nWhich occurrences of the variables in the expression above are free?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $m$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∃:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,n\\,|\\,m\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $m$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃m$ that uses $m:$\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,m\\in Z,\\,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃m}{\\overset{⏞}{n\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∃\\,\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in Z,\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}\\,|\\,{m}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186390441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d432b851-5f5b-5ae5-be26-4e0689222795" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186390443} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d89b72-0360-5da3-ade1-fefd132aa0d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186390444} {:answer/id #uuid "577f3889-2b45-5558-904e-e2cbac624263" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186390445} {:answer/id #uuid "0c6cd02a-2f0d-503e-a37c-d8406949c01d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id 17592186390446} {:answer/id #uuid "f869e96c-c634-5b77-b46e-622984f3215c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186390447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d89b72-0360-5da3-ade1-fefd132aa0d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186390444} :answer-field/id #uuid "323d55c4-1afd-5a97-b428-98053114cd7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186390442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72a396d7-e8cf-5458-9d0a-c10ca03427ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-22984" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n- $4\\pi $\n- $5\\pi $\n- $-2\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id 17592186393133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c33198-cc5c-5160-81c8-f47faca10bd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186393135} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b91d53-b9bb-5ccd-8284-07162ce795a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186393136} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf41b51-bd8b-557e-b601-007569be086e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186393137} {:answer/id #uuid "1f00e675-dab1-5590-88bb-52f5380183c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186393138} {:answer/id #uuid "290fdd35-0379-5b6c-ae69-55ff1d34cf7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186393139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "290fdd35-0379-5b6c-ae69-55ff1d34cf7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186393139} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0197e2-e874-569c-8e06-ebf5b465b2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186393134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89b4a109-08c5-568a-830b-41c72ba008a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71401" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=2$ gives two lines\n- $c=3$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186394023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf467131-ed86-5898-9b24-54a81ef3a425" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186394025} {:answer/id #uuid "408a27ba-3ecd-5304-986f-bfa41f5c422d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186394026} {:answer/id #uuid "d277b1a3-8c90-51e0-95ac-2284987b67c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186394027} {:answer/id #uuid "4e35c7b0-b843-59cc-b0d5-efe5987a56bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186394028} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cd6a57-2825-577b-8bb6-3751ad08ba82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186394029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9cd6a57-2825-577b-8bb6-3751ad08ba82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186394029} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c98af2c-655e-5c74-97bc-4c471b7f7278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186394024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24df999b-6ea6-5095-ada6-d510b0d36076" :question/math-academy-id "q-80652" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186394057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e2e18a-5596-5de6-bbca-4a696f6fe37b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186394059} {:answer/id #uuid "09df3dd0-d105-5273-9da9-f927bd858604" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186394060} {:answer/id #uuid "31ca151e-3d85-538f-9503-8357954047b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186394061} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe4bfb9-4acb-5346-98fc-89085a4ab2ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186394062} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8ec263-0942-5538-bcf0-7ac5209d5ab8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186394063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ca151e-3d85-538f-9503-8357954047b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186394061} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e1a5aa2-c82d-5ff4-92c3-41a98241ecca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186394058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ea0748a-6bf5-5641-b2b0-ad6357ffcd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80757" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+3y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{y}+3x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+xy+5$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y)=3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{y}+3x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2}+{e}^{y})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+xy+5)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({sin}^{2}⁡x+xy-2)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186394064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9df995-ca8a-553b-a6ed-3dd5aef9c75d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186394066} {:answer/id #uuid "274ca2ba-50aa-5d5c-af4b-9697f3a361d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186394067} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b60381-0856-5f13-bfd3-196e04b71673" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186394068} {:answer/id #uuid "6820c28e-c4b3-57ee-827e-0db27a7363cf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186394069} {:answer/id #uuid "c02968a7-a32e-5cbd-9bd2-d22d97951dc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186394070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9df995-ca8a-553b-a6ed-3dd5aef9c75d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186394066} :answer-field/id #uuid "a353388b-c694-5c01-8605-b66eb1aeea2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186394065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "813fc330-f1d0-5e6c-9e4f-df4f6cf7d2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80762" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x-2{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y-2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-2{y}^{2})=-4y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-2xy)=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y})=6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186395991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af31ad74-3d71-5216-82f4-241c0fe0d883" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186395993} {:answer/id #uuid "55344227-ef64-559d-b72e-d67580b900d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186395994} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8d3009-744a-5594-85d0-81623acfa040" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186395995} {:answer/id #uuid "1acecbea-2330-5d66-8f7e-c711b7d73ea2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186395996} {:answer/id #uuid "cabd1c0f-3241-5f02-a31b-66428bbc54cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186395997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af31ad74-3d71-5216-82f4-241c0fe0d883" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186395993} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3af571d-ad32-5475-bbe0-4ca42f82e8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186395992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3861b-a381-5b7c-bc56-9232209a631c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12008" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id 17592186396134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f334c8-c362-5bc3-bb42-acf3a7888901" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186396136} {:answer/id #uuid "306281a3-80c8-5e0a-9a37-cd54c88c73c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186396137} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8c75df-6d72-5de6-8aa7-4da99784ebe0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186396138} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f7d8da-7727-5a58-ab68-96bc81e72057" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186396139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d741b-53e6-5079-a8a5-dd4f4717842f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186396140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306281a3-80c8-5e0a-9a37-cd54c88c73c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186396137} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14f3866-43ea-5b8f-9948-a2dc29883068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2cdae2-5e26-5778-ab7e-65d79d794e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-7639" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Its center is $(1,-3)$\nII. The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$\nIII. The length of the minor axis is $4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false."}] [{:db/id 17592186396162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6a9d072-36c0-5de9-8c2a-721aab34dd27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ only" :db/id 17592186396164} {:answer/id #uuid "610851d8-2303-56fb-9fc0-aefe88bab851" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $L$ only" :db/id 17592186396165} {:answer/id #uuid "25f771aa-b7d7-5d86-a28a-ca24f26dc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id 17592186396166} {:answer/id #uuid "151edfbf-fc56-54ae-9bf2-3f2f8dc0b2de" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id 17592186396167} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb96d3c-a223-5810-9d7d-8755c8adf510" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $M$ only" :db/id 17592186396168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f771aa-b7d7-5d86-a28a-ca24f26dc2ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id 17592186396166} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f553809-f578-5b97-b742-22d7fa6d05fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ba95bcc-83a0-57ed-9254-81c22af4a048" :question/math-academy-id "q-7835" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $K$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $L$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- $M$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $4$ rows and $4$ columns, so it is a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, only $L$ and $M$ are square matrices."}] [{:db/id 17592186396169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb0a517-2a62-5ad4-abb7-32db925dcbb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186396171} {:answer/id #uuid "378c3d9c-f434-5a36-b4c6-e35a124290f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186396172} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb69d4-cf84-531e-a891-dec63188d912" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id 17592186396173} {:answer/id #uuid "e1648b74-cbef-5884-9e5c-c49ee87da068" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id 17592186396174} {:answer/id #uuid "94c1155a-b3ff-59dd-b24f-e9f04aac265d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186396175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c1155a-b3ff-59dd-b24f-e9f04aac265d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id 17592186396175} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5359f3-29e9-58eb-b41e-ef40c0bf51eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17761662-c8e1-5053-be1d-f17ca1632a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7915" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $P$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(P)=4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(P)=0,$ we conclude that $P$ is not invertible. Since $P$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $P$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $Q$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Q)=0,$ we conclude that $Q$ is not invertible. Since $Q$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $Q$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $R$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $Q$ are singular."}] [{:db/id 17592186396176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe865ac-e938-502a-af64-2906292df08f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186396178} {:answer/id #uuid "37f747c4-2d3b-5fc7-9c34-d271a78f7358" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186396179} {:answer/id #uuid "4d878238-fc2c-51a3-9450-0461f9c5a8f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186396180} {:answer/id #uuid "b529905e-a877-50ee-aa7b-eaa49f3e381c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186396181} {:answer/id #uuid "5760bb5a-46a2-50c2-a5f5-3d9d162ce69f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186396182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f747c4-2d3b-5fc7-9c34-d271a78f7358" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186396179} :answer-field/id #uuid "71152846-626d-5dbd-a0ab-1425eb8ae515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72a3fd90-5133-5a81-a0e8-33ac74b2d520" :question/math-academy-id "q-8049" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id 17592186396613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4860770-5064-5335-8f48-a983fc3e37dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186396615} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c0ad1a-1579-5a1d-9f6e-b8c9de4c4be8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186396616} {:answer/id #uuid "776b96b0-8f5c-5b47-aa83-d1f82dd2dcf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186396617} {:answer/id #uuid "36e5fa30-29c5-544a-a26b-8772f9fe51ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186396618} {:answer/id #uuid "1eab1cd1-046e-5de3-9cd8-3170cc92f482" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186396619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eab1cd1-046e-5de3-9cd8-3170cc92f482" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186396619} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cfca3f-c3d8-5194-b838-5df8371ad384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c07f2a41-0b84-51be-b671-ed8827d6cdfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109247" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186396627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bc800b0-3901-5154-81ac-1ef853017320" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Partitions of the Real Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "706a81e0-fa08-57b0-b437-c2f1e80679af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186396629} {:answer/id #uuid "48b2153a-fd69-5239-a9c8-303b9cb48dd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6]$" :db/id 17592186396630} {:answer/id #uuid "cce4d51d-668c-5887-ab39-f0222a35d908" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186396631} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed785a5-44c7-5d1c-ba4b-18ff8aed644c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186396632} {:answer/id #uuid "43a1fd11-acfb-51ce-8e45-54f73c52e999" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186396633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b2153a-fd69-5239-a9c8-303b9cb48dd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6]$" :db/id 17592186396630} :answer-field/id #uuid "813feba2-b955-5d20-b059-a9fb82381546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d62099c6-5c34-55aa-ad1f-5500db9aeb0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122932" :question/problem "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[-4,6]?$\n\nI. $-5<-4<-2<0<4<6$\nII. ${-3,3,5,6}$\nIII. ${-4,4,5}$" :question/worked-solution "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since $-5$ does not belong to $[-4,6].$\n\n- Statement II is not a partition of $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is not a partition of  $[-4,6]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements give a partition of  $[-4,6].$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186396641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b646ac-4e89-5442-9a96-fda0b2f79464" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186396643} {:answer/id #uuid "015a3916-4f00-5a67-a764-b51e236937f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186396644} {:answer/id #uuid "2f030092-da67-5e23-9fdb-56642eb1665c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186396645} {:answer/id #uuid "c378d3ab-a8f5-57f7-bc3c-e72ba32865fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186396646} {:answer/id #uuid "94c121cc-a02d-5f14-83e4-a6c4f52c26fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186396647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c121cc-a02d-5f14-83e4-a6c4f52c26fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186396647} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc69c548-b178-59cf-a7e3-c85b144b3d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186396642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e72e5e7-5d89-539b-aadc-437975100b51" :question/math-academy-id "q-181670" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=1}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =4m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,0,4,8,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186399350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c286d992-e374-59ca-b86a-46b1f01569c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399352} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7fc32d-93c3-5f24-9422-4d81ca03151b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186399353} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb062ee-82cd-5edc-b0e4-91caa68a5294" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186399354} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee651ae-0ee5-5625-ab56-eb885ff22e22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399355} {:answer/id #uuid "eeefac1c-9901-5975-9c13-7fedfd50614c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeefac1c-9901-5975-9c13-7fedfd50614c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399356} :answer-field/id #uuid "027a26b9-e238-53ff-b245-735c0d2abc04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79e7ca1d-1ed8-5888-8523-0ec8ed90db1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101736" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z$\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2(u-1)^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2(2-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2(a-1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{z}^{3}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integral II is divergent."}] [{:db/id 17592186399692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca0c6a8-b2d4-544c-89bf-7a6bdb87675a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186399694} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dff314-51c8-5797-918b-20e449b957ce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186399695} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c19027-5aab-5875-9dab-62a9301471b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186399696} {:answer/id #uuid "aa718d78-9395-5d1b-bac6-f86073e47af5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186399697} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7785c4-6c2c-5f23-92b4-7555437dc106" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id 17592186399698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa718d78-9395-5d1b-bac6-f86073e47af5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186399697} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a779cd-5bb0-57e3-8fd6-1d037d1225d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34a82bff-05f0-5ea6-9b78-0f59cb6e7dc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110294" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id 17592186399898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186166859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb2a3404-f0e3-536c-99b5-77acf32f2b95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186399900} {:answer/id #uuid "8708b279-0ad4-553e-a9d2-8a3c1815eeb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186399901} {:answer/id #uuid "3178b576-e64d-5e6d-8fce-f1c59cc84252" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186399902} {:answer/id #uuid "bb771d15-a098-5767-b90e-f4333e16a09f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399903} {:answer/id #uuid "23b8bc25-3bb3-52b3-8ff8-0407b485e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8708b279-0ad4-553e-a9d2-8a3c1815eeb0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186399901} :answer-field/id #uuid "326fdfde-2910-597f-baa7-9e84efb40a22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53ab2db1-0308-5084-926c-c3a100e39237" :question/math-academy-id "q-116038" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7000c9b2360173996e462883bdf9f89af674b9f099f25a866cd95bd286d9150.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/5f/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c775d264-7add-50d0-9563-a7bc449e39af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186399921} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c7aafc-ae2e-5787-820c-5402603bfece" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399922} {:answer/id #uuid "2824858f-584d-5d06-a903-218ae60d0d62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186399923} {:answer/id #uuid "e2713c21-9042-52eb-96d6-aa1c206f3f35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399924} {:answer/id #uuid "0113c4eb-1180-5aef-8c3f-730b7f7828d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c7aafc-ae2e-5787-820c-5402603bfece" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399922} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e322aae-9a1d-5f87-91ef-3c517a609bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b17707f8-d0a8-5f70-818e-f01d27903349" :question/math-academy-id "q-117010" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$\nII. $(0,0)$\nIII. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97697d4-12b3-5718-85d8-ccf9baa9c0b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399928} {:answer/id #uuid "89b62b05-1225-5e0f-aaa9-52691a529d7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186399929} {:answer/id #uuid "14432c27-d6ae-50d5-8dba-d5691721386e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186399930} {:answer/id #uuid "090ba0b9-9a7f-56da-a9e2-3c7744cb2594" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186399931} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1d97c2-6d62-5f88-b357-30e7e1bfcc80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e1d97c2-6d62-5f88-b357-30e7e1bfcc80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399932} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a03d965-da67-51fe-9c15-60bc125eaf3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d72740e-6072-5046-bfdb-c5667ec877d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117012" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$\nII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186110587 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14166b42-bca6-56b8-a07e-797022314072" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186399956} {:answer/id #uuid "2da35c27-dccd-5d0a-8f1d-bbcd8c3eaa3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399957} {:answer/id #uuid "ac90911c-f7a1-5220-a212-3c6d63800029" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186399958} {:answer/id #uuid "39c54159-c7fd-51f6-9853-fcc01f522381" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399959} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca824dd-135f-51ed-b6ac-7c3e15e12daf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186399960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14166b42-bca6-56b8-a07e-797022314072" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186399956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3edbdcf6-2d84-595e-a82e-d4a8c761ebf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb40c498-010d-593d-917c-a780159dec3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117329" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $-10\\,000$\nII. $\\frac{1}{16}$\nIII. $\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{6}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186399968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa719b7d-d1ee-5649-b870-8f8a3d1177c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186399970} {:answer/id #uuid "18dee54a-fce8-5dc3-a798-772a50cd8901" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186399971} {:answer/id #uuid "730c0a62-4f76-5705-9e0b-4a00253a74f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186399972} {:answer/id #uuid "11d81d17-4b55-5d20-91dc-2a09540e3557" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186399973} {:answer/id #uuid "5bafb7b5-e251-5b4a-b330-592196d2fd1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bafb7b5-e251-5b4a-b330-592196d2fd1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186399974} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f8be92-1eeb-51f2-9522-8aecb4bbebb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d42d29a1-7ec6-555e-8ef7-3216eb563375" :question/math-academy-id "q-117369" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id 17592186400003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36bbe8cc-ddc2-5919-b270-7b19d0e48220" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400005} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb03412-8e94-5385-8192-91a634686b1d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186400006} {:answer/id #uuid "00f5a9e7-5ff9-5cab-b4da-0f197eec9b0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400007} {:answer/id #uuid "7e59753b-1300-571d-af93-523d0d3e5ed0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186400008} {:answer/id #uuid "7632167a-b938-5b51-97c0-caab71755856" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f5a9e7-5ff9-5cab-b4da-0f197eec9b0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400007} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b975aa1-8b82-5577-bfb9-2e2bcc3315d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9e6cde4-30d8-5716-a4a4-97a88908653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120485" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\n\nIII. $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id 17592186400010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e99f652-e012-503c-97ae-9e43e478b1cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400012} {:answer/id #uuid "c83a4309-acbf-5679-9e28-465cf4e9d799" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400013} {:answer/id #uuid "6f833c32-c9f2-5bff-b68d-05c9a5598f8c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186400014} {:answer/id #uuid "8444d1db-6a27-52c8-bd42-58d682c3d6b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400015} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0723d-e0c2-5c94-b776-962ebe8b99f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186400016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e99f652-e012-503c-97ae-9e43e478b1cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400012} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f71519-cda9-5a9b-a1db-b744f73d4f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93bbf63d-41f5-5cec-8b52-34cf770cf20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120614" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n\nIII. $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id 17592186400093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186096740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d14dbef-9a16-537e-a182-87fd6853e543" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecdf47cc-f0cb-56b3-84ca-bee8e2b8bfe4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400095} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbe54b5-3db6-5098-b2a3-f74e2eac1a0a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186400096} {:answer/id #uuid "11b9ceb7-9605-5fd8-a9d6-37030667401b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id 17592186400097} {:answer/id #uuid "4243a661-e4ea-5bc4-a52d-96a26f5bb3ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186400098} {:answer/id #uuid "28ee4859-be0f-5573-bbed-f85800832371" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ee4859-be0f-5573-bbed-f85800832371" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400099} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa5013e-ecbb-5612-b808-2ece59956c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4187c6ec-17d9-5b83-9a20-6aff01cc94de" :question/math-academy-id "q-123315" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $2{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{5}}{2{n}^{5}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2-\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-{n}^{5}}{2-{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}-n)}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-n}{0-1} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence III,  the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{6}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence II diverges."}] [{:db/id 17592186400149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3c60d79-86b9-50cc-9f64-560ea2cecec3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400151} {:answer/id #uuid "04fc169e-d7b2-5fb6-aaaa-03529f82ef7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400152} {:answer/id #uuid "c64b1ae1-73f4-50e4-83d1-af39cac88db8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186400153} {:answer/id #uuid "c0007124-207e-55b6-82bb-cf42de7dce86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400154} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3720ee-33a3-5bc3-abd0-5c477d7133fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d3720ee-33a3-5bc3-abd0-5c477d7133fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186400155} :answer-field/id #uuid "7478616a-dd06-5aea-b75a-b73c688ab5fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55c1789a-3f1b-5ebc-a34c-eaab270f436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124912" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $-1+1-1+1-⋯$\nII. $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$\nIII. $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."}] [{:db/id 17592186400170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95363d69-aef8-5be0-bed6-2e48e831157e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186400172} {:answer/id #uuid "c811cfee-b520-5024-a08a-7253a95a06d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186400173} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4caf99-22b3-54c0-8115-85030a8148f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400174} {:answer/id #uuid "7f361f8f-c150-58fe-9b01-71b7b26b992c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186400175} {:answer/id #uuid "f957daf2-0051-5201-9483-2ef71d3af41d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186400176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4caf99-22b3-54c0-8115-85030a8148f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186400174} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ce3352c-5866-54ec-8397-cf9e37019fd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d26c1fd-9972-5992-b3ee-1cc9fed13d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-125262" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$"}]] "items" [] "response_users" [] "field_owners" [[17592186369299 17592186369298] [17592186369332 17592186369331] [17592186371134 17592186371133] [17592186371168 17592186371167] [17592186371736 17592186371735] [17592186371743 17592186371742] [17592186372154 17592186372153] [17592186372161 17592186372160] [17592186372168 17592186372167] [17592186372175 17592186372174] [17592186372413 17592186372412] [17592186372420 17592186372419] [17592186372427 17592186372426] [17592186372664 17592186372663] [17592186372671 17592186372670] [17592186372678 17592186372677] [17592186372685 17592186372684] [17592186373133 17592186373132] [17592186374262 17592186374261] [17592186374269 17592186374268] [17592186375612 17592186375611] [17592186375688 17592186375687] [17592186375875 17592186375874] [17592186375952 17592186375951] [17592186376048 17592186376047] [17592186376055 17592186376054] [17592186376062 17592186376061] [17592186376069 17592186376068] [17592186376076 17592186376075] [17592186376097 17592186376096] [17592186376104 17592186376103] [17592186376111 17592186376110] [17592186376118 17592186376117] [17592186376708 17592186376707] [17592186377649 17592186377648] [17592186377656 17592186377655] [17592186377785 17592186377784] [17592186377792 17592186377791] [17592186377866 17592186377865] [17592186377873 17592186377872] [17592186377880 17592186377879] [17592186377894 17592186377893] [17592186377970 17592186377969] [17592186377977 17592186377976] [17592186378182 17592186378181] [17592186378189 17592186378188] [17592186378196 17592186378195] [17592186378203 17592186378202] [17592186378210 17592186378209] [17592186378217 1